LS                                                                    LS

NAME
    ls - list directory contents, Unix-style

FORMAT
    ls [-1ahlrRSt] [path | pattern ...]

TEMPLATE
    none - flags are hand-parsed Unix bundles (deliberately not
    ReadArgs); `ls ?` prints usage

PATH
    C:ls

FUNCTION
    Lists directories the way Unix fingers expect: multi-column,
    alphabetical, case-insensitive, dirs and files mixed. A plain
    directory argument lists its contents, a pattern argument lists
    the matches themselves (like ls -d), a plain file lists itself,
    and no argument lists the current directory. Several arguments
    (or -R) get `path:` group headers.

    Multi-column output is sized by asking the console (the CSI
    `0 q` window-bounds request, the same exchange C:Dir uses), so
    it adapts to any conforming console and any window size.
    Redirected or piped output falls back to one entry per line
    with no color codes, so `ls >file` stays parseable.

    On interactive output, directories print blue and hidden-class
    entries (h-bit or .info) grey - grey wins when both apply.

OPTIONS
    -l  long listing: hsparwed protection bits, byte size, DOS
        datestamp, and the filenote on a continuation line
    -a  show .info files and h-bit entries (the Amiga's dotfile
        class - hidden by default)
    -h  human-readable sizes, tiered bytes / K / M / G
    -t  sort by date, newest first
    -S  sort by size, largest first
    -r  reverse the sort order
    -R  recurse into directories, depth-first, with group headers
    -1  one entry per line

RESULTS
    Return code 0 when clean, 5 when a path could not be accessed,
    10 on bad arguments, 20 on Ctrl-C. More than 32 path arguments
    is an error, never a silent truncation.

EXAMPLES
    ls -la work:
    ls -lhS sys:c
    ls #?.e
    ls -R work: >ram:tree.txt

NOTES
    -R never descends soft-links (they list, as Unix ls -R does),
    and a visited-directory set skips hard-link cycles with
    "directory cycle, skipped" instead of looping.

    Empty-named directory entries are never queued by -R (a guard
    from the 0.3.x heap-corruption investigation - see ls-bugs.md
    for that story).

SEE ALSO
    cp.doc, mv.doc, mkdir.doc, rm.doc, tools-audit.md
